#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd806d5-cac7-5ab8-a47e-d04d92de858c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262912} {:answer/id #uuid "3d044d43-04b6-5470-b760-526c2119b7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262913} {:answer/id #uuid "feab9798-b8b0-5866-8c2e-cfeb09826f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262914} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a0c28-1458-5844-8c84-fc64358082b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262915} {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51b0b64-14ee-52f3-8b05-aef2d9f3bc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916} :answer-field/id #uuid "0036bd73-610c-537a-a14e-f81117e7e6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262911}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ced084c-e5cc-510a-ae57-0a31b5977172" :question/math-academy-id "q-112877" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adda1d6a-7132-596e-975a-6b35bfde7502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262919} {:answer/id #uuid "685a3ca8-3740-5aeb-a97a-e7d1b6fa760d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262920} {:answer/id #uuid "cc17ab0e-4d7f-5fb4-b245-11b963aa0357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262921} {:answer/id #uuid "873eaede-5120-5202-8ab0-6ff827aae300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262922} {:answer/id #uuid "88949f27-0690-5d39-bd5b-4325a8444108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873eaede-5120-5202-8ab0-6ff827aae300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262922} :answer-field/id #uuid "65bcd3d2-be94-577a-b059-0f400fb19888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bb110d9-2de4-5865-bc6c-c79c5f02f897" :question/math-academy-id "q-14141" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}+16x-16,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d7feb1-a401-56ee-bb48-e498987e7709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080019} {:answer/id #uuid "a79afe20-f0f9-5ad9-8779-dc823e946ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080020} {:answer/id #uuid "afe771c7-032d-567e-97ea-3ad2a8e50b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080021} {:answer/id #uuid "ff920979-f18a-53f7-a08d-2c07fa7e9702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080022} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3ce6ab-35ae-5c06-ac47-519c40fedd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe771c7-032d-567e-97ea-3ad2a8e50b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080021} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a770f3-b312-5db0-9367-2bb60d923f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080018}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aeb1ad5f-0910-5dea-b64a-e03889cc2cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14145" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = 10x^{6} - 52x^{2} + 50x - 8$, how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c24da-df45-50c7-b327-bf3cb25c2afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "148c24da-df45-50c7-b327-bf3cb25c2afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262926} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b64a15-abf7-58be-9490-19f21b3eaf93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262925}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eef2063a-ea31-5e8a-8994-03247a623cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-270438" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=26{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+4x+4$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $26,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 13,$ and $\\pm 26.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{26}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{13}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{13}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{26},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{13},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc136cb-9ac3-54f1-8bda-c73a5ec5e411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262929} {:answer/id #uuid "56c12eca-e183-58f0-b425-b8d89f27fa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262930} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0a597c-b32e-5a06-a624-0501ec5b7a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262931} {:answer/id #uuid "dec4d002-13f6-5a86-a8b8-93c6e78d53f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262932} {:answer/id #uuid "ab96008f-9b5f-51c8-9afc-ff085673dcfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed0a597c-b32e-5a06-a624-0501ec5b7a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262931} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bba5c28-2c25-5006-bd8e-c67cf9948ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02c855c8-9507-553d-b37d-de70c503e21a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83654" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}]}
